course: dictionary
lesson: 3548
title: Feelers: one word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A feeler is a long, thin part on the head of an insect, snail or other small animal. It is used to touch, smell and sense the world around it. «Like all insects, a beetle has six legs, three body parts and two feelers.» Scientists call insect feelers antennae.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The first meaning is about body parts, but feeler can also be used in an everyday way. Read the words around it to decide which meaning fits.
explain: «To put out feelers» is an informal phrase. It means to quietly try to find out what people think before you decide something. Check by swapping in your meaning: if people are being asked carefully what they think, it is the everyday meaning.
fact: A feeler is a long, thin part on the head of an insect, snail or other small animal.
fact: Feelers are used to touch, smell and sense the world.
fact: Scientists call insect feelers antennae.
fact: Insects have two feelers, as «a beetle has six legs, three body parts and two feelers» shows.
fact: «Put out feelers» means quietly trying to find out what people think before deciding.
exercise: «Like all insects, a beetle has six legs, three body parts and two feelers.» How many feelers does this beetle have? | Six | *Two | Three | Eight
exercise: «Spiders have eight legs, a body in two parts, no feelers and no wings.» Which animal has feelers? | An animal with no legs | *An ant | A spider | A spider with eight eyes
exercise: Scientists call insect feelers by which name? | *Antennae | Eyes | Legs | Wings
test: Nadia says «put out feelers» means to touch something. What does it really mean? | *To quietly try to find out what people think before deciding | To hide in the grass | To grow long, thin legs | To touch something with a hand
test: What does a snail do with its feelers when it is touched? | *Pulls them in | Uses them to fly | Makes them longer | Waves them about faster
exam: Which animal has no feelers? | A beetle | An ant | A butterfly | *A spider
